  "result_text": "**B looks better than A. The estimate is a lift of about 0.63 percentage points, but the interval is wide.**\n\n| | Visits | Signups | Signup rate |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A | 14,089 | 583 | 4.14% |\n| B | 13,961 | 666 | 4.77% |\n\n- **Difference (B \u2212 A):** +0.63 points, which is about a 15% relative lift.\n- **95% confidence interval:** +0.15 to +1.12 points (Wald). A bootstrap that resamples whole days gives +0.18 to +1.10 points. Both exclude zero (z \u2248 2.6).\n- **Range of plausible lift:** the interval corresponds to roughly a 4% to 27% relative lift. The data say B is better, but they don't pin down how much.\n- **Consistency by day:** B beat A on 9 of 14 days, so the gain isn't driven by one or two days.\n- **Data checks:** the file has all 14 days for both variants, no missing or non-numeric values, and visits are balanced between variants (14,089 vs 13,961).\n\n**Caveats**\n- I treated \"randomly assigning each visitor\" as true, which is what lets the difference be read as B's effect. If assignment was actually by day, device or something else, this wouldn't hold.\n- The estimate covers signup rate per visit over these two weeks. It assumes that traffic mix is typical, and it says nothing about signup quality or later retention.\n- You didn't give a practical threshold, such as the smallest lift worth shipping for. If it's above about 0.15 points, the lower end of the interval wouldn't clear it.\n\nI followed the skill's estimation route. The working record is at `/var/folders/4_/s6fbcjyn4b13xdxkd3lv0kw00000gn/T/arm-s9-pre-2-2r1j30ds/work/estimation_record.md`. It's an estimation record rather than a full investigation ledger, so no validator run was owed."
